-- §7  Every remainder, and what its filing established about the set it sits in.
# One row per remainder standing, with how many remainders take it and the filings they sit in.
#
# A remainder needs a closed layer set
#   A remainder is only meaningful if the layer set is closed, and this is where the model says
#   whether anybody closed it. Some remainders sit in a set nobody bounded, which is the honest
#   state for a filing written to argue about three layers, and must never fail anything.
#
# The question was put
#   The assertion is that the question was put, not that it was answered well. A remainder
#   reaching the arithmetic without appearing here at all is the failure; `unbounded` is not.
#
# Driven from the type, not from the rows
#   So a standing nobody holds is a row reading zero. A `GROUP BY` over the remainders can only
#   list the standings some remainder took, and an empty one is exactly the finding a reader of
#   this section would want. `public.remainder_standing` is the declared set, so every member gets
#   a line whether or not the corpus reaches it.
-- public.remainder_standing, each member against layers/remainder_scope.sqlc.
SELECT s.standing::text AS "standing!", count(z.layer) AS "remainders!",
       coalesce(string_agg(DISTINCT z.filing, ', ' ORDER BY z.filing), '') AS "filings!"
FROM unnest(enum_range(NULL::public.remainder_standing)) AS s(standing)
LEFT JOIN (
    :compose(layers/remainder_scope.sqlc)
) z ON z.standing = s.standing
GROUP BY s.standing
ORDER BY count(z.layer) DESC, s.standing
